  "subject": "🫧 Axios Finish Line: Contagious kindness",
  "date": "Wed, 07 Oct 2026 21:29:38 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🦬 Plus: Vista du jour | Wednesday, October 07, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 07, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 381 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: How goodness spreads \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A wealth of psychology and behavioral research lets us track the life cycle of a good deed.\n\n Why it matters: An act of kindness can benefit the person who does it, the person who receives it, and even people who witness it or hear about it. How it works: \n\n 🙋 The doer: Volunteering and performing other acts of kindness — from donating blood to smiling at a stranger on the street — benefits the doer, according to an American Psychological Association review of several research papers. The perks include feeling happier and less lonely, anxious and depressed. 💛 The receiver: As we've reported, we tend to underestimate how much a simple gesture, like holding a door, means to the person on the receiving end. Plus, kindness can set off a chain reaction. People who benefit from a good deed are likelier to direct kindness to strangers themselves, Axios' Ashley May reports . 👀 The witness: Just watching someone do something nice helps, in a phenomenon researchers call \" moral elevation .\" The boost people get from witnessing kindness also makes them likelier to spread it. 📰 Even reading about good deeds has an effect: In a 2023 study published in the journal PLOS ONE , people who read news stories about good deeds after reading negative news stories felt uplifted and more inclined to believe in the goodness of others.\n\n What's next: We want to spread the goodness by reviving one of our favorite Finish Line series — sharing the good deeds you encounter with fellow readers.\n\n Tell us about a random act of kindness you received or witnessed — from a friend, a family member, a neighbor or a total stranger — in 2-3 sentences. Send it to finishline@axios.com, along with your name and hometown, and we'll feature the stories in future editions. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 📸 Magic of the Mountain West \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Aaron Damiani Reader Aaron Damiani of Chicago sends this pic from Fort Smith, a small community in southern Montana, about 90 miles southeast of Billings:\n\n \"I snapped this photo this weekend to share with my wife and kids the beauty of the Montana skies in Fort Smith, right as the sun was setting. In the Adirondack chairs are dear friends who were smoking cigars and taking it all in.\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
